16:53:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right to call that out — if I just say “light doesn’t count because it’s not a physical object,” I’m essentially defining the problem away. That’s not an explanation, that’s a dismissal. Let’s go deeper into the actual physics, no circular definitions. The sound barrier is **not a speed limit** — it’s a **wave-interference effect**. Here’s the causal chain: 1. **A moving object creates a series of sound waves.** Every time it pushes on an air molecule, that molecule bumps into the next, sending out a spherical pressure pulse (a sound wave). A jet continuously pushes air aside, so it emits a continuous train of these pulses, all traveling outward at the speed of sound from wherever the jet was when it emitted them. 2. **If the object moves slower than sound**, those spherical waves all stay nested inside each other, never catching up. They spread out harmlessly, and an observer hears a normal, non-boom sound. 3. **If the object moves exactly at the speed of sound**, it constantly catches up to its own waves. The wave fronts pile up exactly on top of each other, forming a wall of pressure — that’s the shockwave. As the object breaks through, you hear the “boom” because all those piled-up wave fronts hit you at once. 4. **If the object moves faster than sound**, the piled-up waves trail behind it in a cone (Mach cone), and you still get a boom when that cone passes you. So **the entire phenomenon depends on the object producing sound waves as it moves.** If an object **does not generate sound waves** at all, there is nothing to pile up, no matter how fast it goes. It’s not that light is “excused” from the barrier; it’s that the barrier never even gets a chance to form because step 1 doesn’t happen. Now the key question: **Does light generate sound waves when it moves through air? Does it push on air molecules?** Physically, no. - Light is an oscillation of electric and magnetic fields. When it passes by an air molecule, the molecule’s